{"tool_name":"capice","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"CAPICE","home":"https://molgenis43.gcc.rug.nl/","summary":"A computational method for Consequence-Agnostic Pathogenicity Interpretation of Clinical Exome variations.","addition_date":"2020-01-14T09:14:00Z","last_update_date":"2021-01-14T17:14:55Z","tool_type":["Web application"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:CAPICE","edit_permission":{"type":"public"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Variant classification","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3225"},{"term":"Variant prioritisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3226"},{"term":"Virulence prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3461"}]}],"name":"CAPICE","owner":"Pub2Tools","publication":[{"doi":"10.1101/19012229"},{"doi":"10.1186/s13073-020-00775-w","metadata":{"abstract":"\u00a9 2020 The Author(s).Exome sequencing is now mainstream in clinical practice. However, identification of pathogenic Mendelian variants remains time-consuming, in part, because the limited accuracy of current computational prediction methods requires manual classification by experts. Here we introduce CAPICE, a new machine-learning-based method for prioritizing pathogenic variants, including SNVs and short InDels. CAPICE outperforms the best general (CADD, GAVIN) and consequence-type-specific (REVEL, ClinPred) computational prediction methods, for both rare and ultra-rare variants. CAPICE is easily added to diagnostic pipelines as pre-computed score file or command-line software, or using online MOLGENIS web service with API. Download CAPICE for free and open-source (LGPLv3) at https://github.com/molgenis/capice.","authors":[{"name":"Abbott K."},{"name":"Charbon B."},{"name":"De Ridder D."},{"name":"Deelen P."},{"name":"Hendriksen D."},{"name":"Kerstjens-Frederikse W.S."},{"name":"Li S."},{"name":"Sikkema-Raddatz B."},{"name":"Sinke R.J."},{"name":"Soudis D."},{"name":"Swertz M.A."},{"name":"Van Der Velde K.J."},{"na